SSH in

There is a setuid binary in the homedirectory that does the following: it makes a connection to localhost on the port you specify as a commandline argument. 
It then reads a line of text from the connection and compares it to the password in the previous level (bandit20). 
If the password is correct, it will transmit the password for the next level (bandit21).

NOTE: Try connecting to your own network daemon to see if it works as you think

This seems a bit more complex to understand, to make it simpler, we are opening a port on our local host and setting it to listen to a connection.
The port will take a commandline arguemnet, which will be sent by the setuid binary. The port will echo the last levels password.
If the password matches what the setuid binary has it will output the correct password.

Check folder.
    bandit20@bandit:~$ ls
    suconnect

Lets build the port. 
We will use netcat to create the port.
       -l      Listen for an incoming connection rather than initiating a connection to a remote host.  The destination and port to listen  on
               can  be specified either as non-optional arguments, or with options -s and -p respectively.  Cannot be used together with -x or
               -z.  Additionally, any timeouts specified with the -w option are ignored.

so we want this port to output the password for the previous level, we will use echo
When using echo normally, it will create a trailing line which would create an error for the suconnect e.g

    bandit20@bandit:~$ echo KpsOfPkcP7i1FlIExk2QEjyt6dw8dxZI
    KpsOfPkcP7i1FlIExk2QEjyt6dw8dxZI
    bandit20@bandit:~$ echo -n  KpsOfPkcP7i1FlIExk2QEjyt6dw8dxZI
    KpsOfPkcP7i1FlIExk2QEjyt6dw8dxZIbandit20@bandit:~$ 

We then take the echo and pipe it into the Netcat open port command
    bandit20@bandit:~$ echo -n KpsOfPkcP7i1FlIExk2QEjyt6dw8dxZI | nc -l -p 1212

This however stops us from using the terminal, so we need a method to run the NC command in the background.
This is done with the & modifier.
    bandit20@bandit:~$ echo -n KpsOfPkcP7i1FlIExk2QEjyt6dw8dxZI | nc -l -p 1212 &
    [2] 257
Now we see the port is open on 1212 lets connect to it with suconnect.
    bandit20@bandit:~$ ./suconnect 1212
    Read: KpsOfPkcP7i1FlIExk2QEjyt6dw8dxZI
    ERROR: This doesn't match the current password!
    FAIL!
    [2]+  Done                       echo -n KpsOfPkcP7i1FlIExk2QEjyt6dw8dxZI | nc -l -p 1212
We get this error as the password was not enclosed in '' making it count the spaces as part of the password.

